ColumNAR.blocks.bytes_fetched|BYTES|null|null|Stored size of the blocks that were fetched, compressed or not, counted again for a block fetched again. It is read-time activity, not the size of the column on disk, and it is the codec's input, not file or network reads.
ColumNAR.blocks.bytes_inflated|BYTES|null|null|Size of the blocks that were inflated, once inflated. Their stored size is in blocks.bytes_fetched.
ColumNAR.blocks.decode_cpu_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|ADAPTIVE|ColumNAR.blocks.decode_time_nanos|CPU time the executing thread spent in the calls that blocks.decode_time_nanos covers, an estimate from the same sample of the calls. The elapsed time minus this is an approximate off-CPU component: waits such as page faults, scheduling or blocking, without saying which. CPU time includes instructions executed while the processor stalls on memory, so it does not expose cache or memory stalls, and it can include kernel execution. Present only when the collector measures thread CPU time and the platform's clock stayed valid. The search and ES|QL profiles do not ask for thread CPU time, so it is not reported there.
ColumNAR.blocks.decode_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|ADAPTIVE|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of the calls that decoded a fetched block into values, an estimate from a sample of the calls once there are many. A block stored plain is read off the file while it is decoded, so the time can include waits for the data; a block that was inflated first is decoded from memory. It follows the fetch that blocks.fetch_time_nanos covers and does not overlap it. It is not CPU time.
ColumNAR.blocks.decompress_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|EXACT|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of the calls that inflated a block, measured exactly. A block stored plain is not timed. It is part of the time of fetching the block, so do not add it to blocks.fetch_time_nanos. It is not CPU time.
ColumNAR.blocks.decompressed|COUNT|null|null|Blocks, among those fetched, that were stored compressed and were inflated. A block stored plain is not counted. This is the compression of integer blocks; the chunks of a byte stream are counted by chunks.decompressed, and no byte is in both.
ColumNAR.blocks.fetch_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|ADAPTIVE|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of the calls that fetched a block, an estimate from a sample of the calls once there are many. It runs from the call until the block is available, so it includes inflating a compressed block and any wait for the data, and it leaves out decoding the block afterwards. A call that threw is included. It is not CPU time. It contains blocks.decompress_time_nanos, so do not add the two or subtract one from the other: an estimate can be lower than an exact time it contains.
ColumNAR.blocks.fetched|COUNT|null|null|Blocks of an integer stream that were fetched because the reader did not hold them: ordinals, length codes and slot counts of a string column, or values of a numeric column, all counted together. A reader holds one block of each stream, so a block it needs again after moving on is fetched and counted again. A block the reader already holds is not counted.
ColumNAR.blocks.values_decoded|COUNT|null|null|Integer values decoded from the blocks that were fetched: a whole block's worth for each fetch, however few of them the read needed, and again for a block fetched again. They are the values of every integer stream of the column together: ordinals, length codes and slot counts of a string column, never its value bytes. Only for a column of one such stream, a numeric column or a dictionary column of single values, does comparing it with the values a read returned approximate decode amplification, and the ratio is not an exact factor.
ColumNAR.chunks.bytes_compressed|BYTES|null|null|Stored size of the chunks that were inflated. This is the decompressor's input, not file or network reads.
ColumNAR.chunks.bytes_inflated|BYTES|null|null|Size of the chunks that were inflated, including the bytes of a chunk that the read did not use. This is the compression of a byte stream's chunks: values, escaped values, the summary. Integer blocks are counted by blocks.decompressed, and no byte is in both. Read-time activity, not the size of the column.
ColumNAR.chunks.cache_hits|COUNT|null|null|Times a read needed a chunk and the reader already held it, so nothing was inflated. Together with chunks.decompressed this is every chunk the reads needed. A read that spans several chunks needs each of them once.
ColumNAR.chunks.decompress_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|EXACT|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of the calls that inflated a chunk, measured exactly. It covers the decompressor call only, and a call that threw is included. It is not CPU time and it can include waits for data. A chunk inflated for a page is part of pages.call_time_nanos or pages.byte_lengths_call_time_nanos, so do not add it to those.
ColumNAR.chunks.decompressed|COUNT|null|null|Compressed chunks of a column's values that were inflated. Reading the chunk a reader already holds is not counted.
ColumNAR.dictionary.resolve_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|ADAPTIVE|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of the dictionary term lookups, an estimate from a sample of the calls once there are many. A lookup reads the term where it lies in the file, so the time can include waits for the data, such as page faults on a mapped file. The uncompressed reads and block fetches a lookup triggers count into their own metrics too, so do not add this time to theirs. It is not CPU time.
ColumNAR.dictionary.term_lookups|COUNT|null|null|Terms resolved from the dictionary, once per lookup. Reading a value one document at a time, bisecting for a term, a range or a prefix, and building the terms of a page each resolve terms; a term served from bytes the reader already holds still counts as a lookup. An escaped value is not a term and is not counted.
ColumNAR.ordinal_skip.docid_span|DOC_IDS|null|null|Document ids covered by the spans an ordinal range stepped over, whether or not each id holds a value.
ColumNAR.ordinal_skip.intervals_rejected|COUNT|null|null|Spans of documents an ordinal range stepped over because the skip index showed no ordinal in them can match.
ColumNAR.ordinal_skip.navigation_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|ADAPTIVE|null|Elapsed wall-clock time spent stepping over spans of an ordinal range, an estimate from a sample of the calls once there are many. It includes the advances of the underlying iterator and any wait for data. It is the cost of the navigation, not the time it saved. It is not CPU time.
ColumNAR.pages.as_ordinals|COUNT|null|null|Pages of a string column that were handed over as ordinals into a dictionary of their own. Counted when the page begins.
ColumNAR.pages.as_values|COUNT|null|null|Pages of a string column that were handed over as their values, for pages that repeat too little for ordinals. Empty pages are not counted. Counted when the page begins.
ColumNAR.pages.byte_lengths_call_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|EXACT|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of the calls that ask a string column for the byte lengths of a page of values, as BYTE_LENGTH does, measured exactly. It covers the whole call, including the fetches, inflations and dictionary lookups that have metrics of their own, and a call that threw. No page is handed over, so these calls are not in pages.call_time_nanos and move no page counter. It is not CPU time.
ColumNAR.pages.call_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|EXACT|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of the calls that ask a string column for a page of values, measured exactly, whatever the call did. It covers the whole call: finding the documents' values, the fetches, inflations and dictionary lookups that have metrics of their own, the work that has none, building the page, and the consumer's own work on the page it is handed. A call that declined the page, which hands nothing over, and a call that threw are included, so its calls can outnumber the pages counted by pages.as_ordinals and pages.as_values. It is not CPU time and it can include waits for data. It contains the time of the nested metrics, so do not add it to theirs.
ColumNAR.pages.values_in_ordinal_pages|COUNT|null|null|Values carried by the pages that were handed over as ordinals.
ColumNAR.pages.values_in_value_pages|COUNT|null|null|Values carried by the pages that were handed over as values.
ColumNAR.readers.dictionary|COUNT|null|null|String columns opened for reading that name their values with ordinals into a dictionary.
ColumNAR.readers.numeric|COUNT|null|null|Numeric columns opened for reading.
ColumNAR.readers.open_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|EXACT|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of requests for a column, measured exactly. It covers building the reader and nothing the reader does afterwards. It is not CPU time and it can include waits for data.
ColumNAR.readers.opened|COUNT|null|null|Columns opened for reading, once for each request for a column. Readers are not reused across requests. It equals readers.plain plus readers.dictionary plus readers.numeric.
ColumNAR.readers.plain|COUNT|null|null|String columns opened for reading that store their values one after another, without a dictionary.
ColumNAR.uncompressed.bytes_read|BYTES|null|null|Bytes the uncompressed reads copied off the file. This is the reads' input, not file or network reads.
ColumNAR.uncompressed.read_time_nanos|TIME_NANOS|ADAPTIVE|null|Elapsed wall-clock time of the uncompressed reads, an estimate from a sample of the calls once there are many. It covers the copy off the file and can include waits for the data, such as page faults on a mapped file; for a stream stored uncompressed this is its whole storage cost. It can run inside pages.call_time_nanos, pages.byte_lengths_call_time_nanos or dictionary.resolve_time_nanos, so do not add it to those. It is not CPU time.
ColumNAR.uncompressed.reads|COUNT|null|null|Bulk reads of a stream stored uncompressed, one for each contiguous range read off the file. A read served from the one chunk the reader holds is not counted. The dictionary of a string column is always stored uncompressed.
